# De Dion-Bouton Type V tonneau, 1902-1908.
#
# The archetypal early French light car, and the make that did most to put
# motorists on the road in Britain before 1910: De Dion-Bouton sold through its
# own British syndicate, and the marque appears throughout the British price
# lists of the period. The Type V is a small single-cylinder tonneau with a
# tiller, wooden artillery wheels and two rows of seats.
# https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/De_Dion-Bouton
#
# No works dimensions have been found for the tonneau body. The model was built
# to these ESTIMATES: about 2.900 m long, 1.400 m wide, 1.750 m high with the
# hood raised, 1.905 m wheelbase. Correct them if a source is found.
#
# Colours: Cream, Dark Green, Maroon and Black are documented period finishes;
# the museum car in the references is cream with a maroon chassis.
#
# Weight: 6 in total (cream 2, green 2, maroon 1, black 1). The French light car
# was numerous in Britain but the register treats imports as representative.
#
# Speed 45 km/h; comfort 25/220 (line). Class 2.
# Dates: 1902-1908 with a road tail to about 1928.
#
# Metres master: citycars/4.5/de-dion-type-v.blend. References:
# citycars/references/de-dion-type-v/ (gitignored). Images via Wikimedia Commons.

obj=citycar
name=DeDionTypeV(Cream)
copyright=JamesPetts

DistributionWeight=2
intro_year=1902
intro_month=5
retire_year=1928
retire_month=6
speed=45
class=2
maximum_comfortable_journey_minutes=25
maximum_tolerable_journey_minutes=220

